# HAKEM 2, 2026-08-30 — THE SHIPPED PATH'S OWN MEDIAN, WHICH 2b HAS NEVER MEASURED.
#
# Instrument: the SAME R1.3 in-process probe reports/R7/accept.sh mandates
# (a copy of native/gate.cpp patched under /tmp with steady_clock at the top of
# main() + atexit dump, compiled there; native/ untouched). Same event, same
# warmup (60) and same sample count (300) as accept.sh's 2b arm.
#
# THE ONLY DIFFERENCE FROM 2b: no daemon, and RABADON_GATED_SOCK unset.
# That is the configuration every hook on the operator's machine runs in:
#   ~/.claude/settings.json registers native/rabadon-gate on 5 events.
#   It registers rabadon-gated on ZERO events.
#   No launchd plist, no running rabadon-gated process.
#   gated_client.h:69 resolve_sock() -> $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR or /tmp + rabadon-<uid>.sock
#   /tmp/rabadon-501.sock EXISTS (24 Aug 15:29) with NO listener, so this
#   measurement INCLUDES the dead connect() the operator pays today.

SHIPPED-PATH (no daemon) n=300 median=766.3 us  p90=861.0  min=664.5  max=2436.1

CEILING = 1000 us.  766.3 < 1000.  The path the user actually runs is UNDER it.

For comparison, from the first arbiter's run (kanit/f3j-hakem/hakem-accept.out):
  FAIL 2b the gate's median is 2009.0 us with the daemon up, ceiling is 1000 us

  daemon up   : 2009.0 us
  daemon absent:  766.3 us
  the daemon COSTS +1242.7 us, i.e. 2.62x. It is not a speedup, it is the defect.

MECHANISM, from the tree's own words (native/gate.cpp:538):
  "rabadon-gated forks a worker per request, so every request paid the cold
   price again"
  native/gated.cpp:269 and :294 — two fork() calls per served request.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PAIRED VERSION, BECAUSE THE NUMBER ABOVE IS CROSS-SESSION AND THAT IS THE
# EXACT SIN THREE ARBITERS BEFORE ME WERE CALLED OUT FOR.
# Same probe, same event, ABBA ordering (D,N,N,D per rep) so the machine's
# monotonic drift cancels instead of loading one arm. 400 samples per arm per
# rep, both arms in ONE sitting, daemon started in a sandbox HOME with an
# explicit short socket and killed after; the socket was removed.

rep1 (n=400/arm): daemon UP 1661.3 us | daemon DOWN 1023.1 us | daemon costs  +638.2
rep2 (n=400/arm): daemon UP 1924.5 us | daemon DOWN 1142.3 us | daemon costs  +782.1
rep3 (n=400/arm): daemon UP 1977.2 us | daemon DOWN 1282.7 us | daemon costs  +694.6

mean = +705.0 us, sign 3 plus / 0 minus.

VERDICT: the daemon is a REGRESSION of about +705 us, measured paired, and the
sign is unanimous. It is not "not yet fast enough" - it is the single largest
term in 2b's failure, and it is a component NOTHING SHIPS.

LOAD CAVEAT, STATED HONESTLY: these absolute numbers are higher than the 766.3
us at the top because the machine was busy with this session's own work by then
(the daemon-DOWN arm reads 1023-1283 us here vs 766.3 idle). The 766.3 us figure
is the idle one and it is the one to compare against the 1000 us ceiling; the
+705 us figure is the load-controlled one and it is the one to compare against
zero. Both point the same way. I did NOT re-run the idle measurement under load
control, so the single number "766.3" carries an idle-machine assumption.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT I COULD NOT MEASURE: the stale socket's cost (the card's open item #1).
# /tmp/rabadon-501.sock has existed with no listener since 24 Aug, so every gate
# call stats it, connects, and is refused. I tried to size it by comparing the
# stale socket VISIBLE against XDG_RUNTIME_DIR pointed at an empty directory:
#   rep1 786.2 vs 1066.8 | rep2 945.0 vs 1000.4 | rep3 996.4 vs 1046.1 | rep4 1103.8 vs 1134.5
#   mean -104.1 us, 0 plus / 4 minus -- i.e. "removing the dead socket makes it
#   SLOWER", which is physically absurd.
# The artifact is visible in the data: within a single arm the medians climb
# monotonically (786 -> 945 -> 996 -> 1104), the machine was drifting, and I ran
# A-then-B in every rep so B always landed later. NOT AN ABBA DESIGN. The result
# is an ordering artifact, not a measurement.
# I DID NOT MEASURE THE STALE SOCKET'S COST. Same as the card. I did not delete
# the socket either - it is the operator's file.
